136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (A pharmacy calculates a bill from price and quantity, applies a 15% senior-citizen discount, adds 5% GST, and gives Rs. 50 off when the total exceeds Rs. 1000. The supplied expression has precedence, associativity and integer-division errors.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (Multiplication and division have higher precedence than addition and subtraction. Operators at the same precedence are evaluated left to right. Integer division truncates fractions. The conditional operator ?: selects one of two values. Post-increment yields the old value and then increments; an unsequenced modification can cause undefined behaviour.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (The corrected calculation applies discount before GST and applies the threshold reduction afterward. The three predicted results are consistent with the corrected formula.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (The original expression evaluates multiplication first, then left-to-right. In 15/100 with integer constants, division becomes zero, so the intended 15% discount is lost; similarly 5/100 becomes zero. Use 15.0/100.0 or 0.15. The expression count = count++ + 1 modifies count more than once without sequencing, so its behaviour is undefined; -Wall may warn about sequence-point issues. Intermediate variables improve readability and correctness; operation count remains O\(1\), with only O\(1\) extra space.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
